# Ignore RStudio junk
.Rproj.user/
.Rhistory
*.Rhistory
.RData
.RDataTmp*

# Ignore large binary files
*.RData
*.rds
*.fst
*.csv
*.tsv
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.sav
*.dta
*.zip
*.txt
*.xml
*.pdf
*.docx
*.png
*.jpg

# Ignore project output folders
chunk_outputs/
meta_outputs_fst/
PUMAFIles/
PUMS to IPUMS/
*.html
*.pdf
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.tif
*.tiff


# Keep only R scripts and project metadata
!*.Rproj
!README.md
.positai

# Modular pipeline (scripts/data_construction/, scripts/analysis/): the .R
# files themselves are meant to be tracked. Their runtime checkpoint images
# (checkpoints/after_secXX.RData in each half) are already caught by the
# *.RData rule above -- this line is just documentation, not a new pattern.
# scripts/*/checkpoints/

# Dissertation deliverables.
# The blanket *.pdf / *.png rules above exist to keep rendered drafts and data
# dumps out of the repo, and they also caught every figure the chapters show.
# The figures and tables the .tex files \includegraphics and \input are
# deliverables rather than by-products, so they are re-included here. These
# negations work because outputs/ is not itself an excluded directory -- git
# cannot re-include a file beneath an excluded parent, so if outputs/ is ever
# added to the folder list above, these lines stop having any effect.
#
# Only the two deliverable folders are re-included. Note that the map PDFs are binaries of
# a few hundred KB each: every re-run of 28 or 29 rewrites them in full, so
# each run adds that much to history rather than a diff.
!outputs/figures/*.pdf
!outputs/figures/*.png
# .tex was never matched by a rule above; listed for intent, not effect.
!outputs/tables/*.tex
# The estimation results the rendering scripts read. 292 KB for all of them,
# and committing them makes each rendered table auditable against its source
# rather than only reproducible by re-running the estimation.
!outputs/tables/*.csv


# Inputs the pipeline cannot run without.
# The blanket *.xml and *.csv rules above exist to keep data extracts out, but
# they also caught two small files the code reads directly, so a fresh clone
# could not run: 00_setup_analysis.R parses the DDI to build bpld_lookup, and
# 29_map_destination_us.R reads the county crosswalk. Both are reference
# files of under a megabyte, not data, and both are re-included here.
!data/crosswalks/*
# 02_msa_crosswalk.R reads six files from data/crosswalks/ and only one was
# tracked, so the whole folder is re-included -- 5.2 MB of published PUMA,
# CBSA and county reference tables, all Census Bureau products.
# data/raw_pums_1990/ stays ignored: it is 1.2 GB and the only script that
# ever read it is archived and unused.
#
# The IPUMS DDI codebook is NOT re-included. IPUMS bars redistribution of its
# microdata, and whether that extends to the codebook documenting an extract
# is not something we could establish. It is also unnecessary: anyone
# reproducing this must build their own extract, which generates its own DDI.
# The extract specification is in the README so an equivalent one can be
# built. outputs/tables/bpld_codes.csv is excluded for the same reason -- it
# is a cache of IPUMS value labels, rebuilt by 22_origin_composition.R.
usa_00007.xml
usa_00007.cbk
Codebooks/
outputs/tables/bpld_codes.csv

# Superseded code and workflow plumbing. archive/ holds 67 files recording
# every approach tried and abandoned, and tools/ holds the sync scripts for a
# two-repo setup that no longer exists. Neither helps anyone reproduce the
# analysis, both publish abandoned avenues as though they were closed, and
# several files hardcode local OneDrive paths and usernames
# (archive/scripts/Immigration_Card_Data_In.R, tools/archive/sync_chapters.R).
archive/
tools/

# IPUMS microdata extracts. Large, and IPUMS requires each user to register
# and generate their own, so they cannot be redistributed here anyway.
*.gz
*.dat

# Private working notes. The repo is going public; the tracked scripts state
# what the design is and why, and notes/methods_history.md keeps the record of
# what was tried and set aside. That record stays out of the public artefact
# because "we tried X and it broke" reads as a verdict on X when it is usually
# a verdict on one implementation whose cause was never isolated, and would
# discourage someone from an avenue that is not actually closed.
notes/

# The working paper and the script that builds it. The paper is a generated
# repackaging of Immigration_Card.tex for circulation, not a research artefact:
# tracking it would put a second copy of the chapter in the repo and invite the
# two to drift, and the build script is LaTeX plumbing that asserts on the
# chapter's exact comment text so it fails loudly when the chapter changes.
# Neither is any use to someone reproducing the analysis, which is what this
# repo is for. The committee receives the PDF. The build script lives in
# notes/, already excluded above, so that scripts/ holds only the pipeline.
chapter2_working_paper.tex
chapter2_jmp.tex

# Internal working documents and superseded drafts. The repo is going public as
# a replication artefact: it carries the pipeline, the outputs it produces, and
# the chapter those outputs appear in. Process notes, review passes, chapter
# assignments and earlier drafts of the prose are working material, and
# dissertation_2026-10-05.tex contains unpublished Chapter 1.
CHAPTER_ASSIGNMENT.md
Chapter3_Context_Document.md
TEX_REVIEW_ch2_2026-10-04.md
writeup_notes_2026-09-22.md
ch2_conclusion_draft.tex
ch2_results_excerpt.tex
ch2_robustness_excerpt.tex
chapter2_immigration.tex
dissertation_2026-10-05.tex
